    "query": "Huawei entity list impact China rare earth minerals retaliation June 2019",
    "search_window": {
      "from": "2019-04-06",
      "to": "2019-06-05"
    },
    "sources": {
      "grounding": {
        "generic": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.scmp.com/news/china/diplomacy/article/3012287/chinese-media-raise-rare-earths-threats-after-us-ups-trade-war",
            "title": "China issues veiled rare earths threats after US ups trade war ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# China issues veiled rare earths threats after US ups trade war ante with Huawei blacklist\nPublished: 5:30pm, 29 May 2019 Updated: 9:37am, 30 May 2019\n\nChina has issued veiled threats that the country could retaliate against the US by restricting rare earths exports after US President Donald Trump imposed new measures to cut off the supply of chips and processors to Chinese telecoms giant Huawei",
              "{\"alternativeHeadline\":\"China issues veiled rare earths threats after US Huawei blacklist\",\"articleBody\":\"On Wednesday, Lu said the NDRC “is a Chinese government body – its statement is certainly authoritative”. The NDRC statement was the first official comment by the Chinese government on the controversial issue amid growing speculation that China might weaponise its rare earths in its trade war with the United States. But Lu’s remarks were in contrast to his answer last week when he was asked to comment on a high-profile visit by President Xi Jinping to a rare earths producer in the eastern province of Jiangxi during which the Chinese leader emphasised the strategic value of the metals. “Inspection by China’s state leaders to relevant industries is very normal,” Lu said then. “Please do not read too much into it.” Lu’s tougher comments echoed a more strident rallying call carried in China’s official media on Wednesday warning the US not to underestimate Beijing’s resolve in striking back, and that the US had “completely overestimated its ability to manipulate the global supply chain”. “The Chinese people would never agree to the US using products made from rare earths exported from China to curb and suppress China’s development,” Communist Party mouthpiece People’s Daily said in a commentary. “We remind the US not to underestimate China’s ability to defend its rights to development and to remember not to blame anyone for not warning them.” China will not rule out using rare earth exports as leverage in trade war with US China accounts for 90 per cent of the global supply of the minerals, which are critical to the manufacture of consumer electronics, semiconductors and military equipment. China and the US have battled over trade for nearly a year, casting a long shadow over the world economy and straining ties between the two countries. Last week, the White House added Huawei Technologies, the world’s biggest telecommunications equipment manufacturer, to a trading blacklist. Several global companies have since suspended business with the Chinese firm, raising fears that the ban will seriously undermine China’s burgeoning chip industry. Meanwhile official Chinese news agency Xinhua said there were some “delusional” American politicians who thought they could curb China’s rise through limiting technology cooperation with China. Explainer: Can the US take back some control of rare earths from China? Hawkish state-run tabloid the Global Times ran a separate article saying the comments by the NDRC on Tuesday were a “clear signal” that it was only a matter of time for Beijing to play the “rare earths card” in its feud with the US. “China understands clearly that the complex global supply chain comprises industries from every country in the world,” it said. “If China cuts off the exports of rare earths to the US … then China will also suffer but the impact felt by the US would be much more serious. “We believe if the US continues to pile pressure on us, it would be just a matter of time that we will weaponise our [rare earths exports].” China was accused of making a similar move in 2010 when tensions with Japan rose over a territorial dispute in the East China Sea. Observers say Beijing risks undermining its trading reputation and other trading partners might suffer if China restricts exports of the minerals.\",\"articleSection\":\"Diplomacy\",\"author\":{\"name\":\"Laura Zhou\",\"sameAs\":\"https://www.scmp.com/author/laura-zhou\"},\"dateCreated\":\"2019-05-29T16:57:53+08:00\",\"dateModified\":\"2019-05-30T09:37:33+08:00\",\"datePublished\":\"2019-05-29T17:30:09+08:00\",\"genre\":\"News\",\"hasPart\":{\"isAccessibleForFree\":\"False\"},\"headline\":\"China issues veiled rare earths threats after US ups trade war ante with Huawei blacklist\",\"inLanguage\":\"en-GB\",\"isAccessibleForFree\":\"False\",\"locationCreated\":{\"name\":\"Hong Kong\"},\"publisher\":{\"name\":\"South China Morning Post\"}}"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.theverge.com/2019/5/31/18647021/china-us-trade-war-trump-huawei-rare-earths-embargo-softbank-5g-snub",
            "title": "China prepares to strike back at US as Huawei suffers another loss ...",
            "snippets": [
              "China is taking steps to respond to the American ban on doing business with Huawei. Bloomberg reports that China has put preparations in place to restrict exports of rare earth minerals to the US, while also setting up its own “unreliable entities” blacklist for unfavorable foreign companies. At the same time, Japan’s SoftBank is snubbing Huawei 5G equipment.",
              "# China prepares to strike back at US as Huawei suffers another loss\nThe trade tension between the United States and China looks set to intensify soon, as the latter country is taking steps to respond to the American ban on doing business with Huawei. Bloomberg reports that China has put preparations in place to restrict exports of rare earth minerals to the US, while also setting up its own “unreliable entities” blacklist for unfavorable foreign companies",
              "### Related\nAs to the Chinese entity list, this echoes the language of the US Bureau of Industry and Security’s Entity List on which Huawei finds itself now. That could well be a deliberate sign of a tit-for-tat response, as China doesn’t really need a formal list, having already imposed bans on a number of prominent US businesses like Google and Facebook",
              "The list will cover “foreign enterprises, organizations and individuals that don’t obey market rules, violate contracts and block, cut off supply for non-commercial reasons or severely damage the legitimate interests of Chinese companies,” according to China National Radio, citing a government official. In simple terms, China is mirroring the actions of the US and taking on a more assertive posture as it heads into its next round of negotiations"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.businessinsider.com/us-huawei-ban-reaction-major-incoming-state-media-2019-5",
            "title": "China is reportedly on the brink of 'major retaliative measures' ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# China is reportedly on the brink of 'major retaliative measures' against the US as the trade war escalates further\nChina is preparing to deploy \"major retaliative measures\" over the US' decision to blacklist Huawei, according to the editor of one of the country's state media outlets.\n\nHu Xijin, editor of the tabloid newspaper Global Times, wrote on Twitter: \"China will take major retaliative measures against the US placing Huawei and other Chinese companies on Entity List.\"",
              "\"This move indicates Beijing will not wait passively and more countermeasures will follow.\"\n\nOn Friday, China's Ministry of Commerce said it will set up an \"unreliable entities list\" which will \"target foreign firms that cut supplies to China,\" Reuters reported",
              "Huawei's Chief Legal Officer Song Liuping speaks during a press conference in Shenzhen, China's Guangdong province on March 7, 2019.  WANG ZHAO/AFP/Getty Images\n\n140 other Chinese companies were also added to the list. China claims Huawei is independent of the government",
              "### Explore BI Games\nPlay now\n\nIn retaliation, China is expected to restrict the export of rare earth metals to the US. On Tuesday, Hu tweeted: \"China is seriously considering restricting rare earth exports to the US.\"",
              "Rumours China would restrict US access to rare earths in response to the Huawei ban circulated after Chinese President Xi Jinping made a highly-publicized visit to a rare earth factory in eastern China.\n\n***Read more:** US companies pay 'almost entirely' for tariffs on Chinese products, new IMF study shows — blowing a massive hole in Trump's favorite justification for the trade war*"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://en.businesstimes.cn/articles/112927/20190530/china-retaliates-rare-earths-over-u-s-ban-huawei-supply.htm",
            "title": "China Retaliates With Rare Earths Over U.S. Ban Of Huawei Supply ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# China Retaliates With Rare Earths Over U.S. Ban Of Huawei Supply\nHuawei filed a summary motion today as part of the process to challenge the constitutionality of Section 889 of the National Defense Authorization Act (NDAA) of 2019, and called on the United States government to Stop the smear campaign against them. (Photo: REUTERS/Edgar Su/File Photo)\n\nAs the trade war rages, China might leverage its abundance of rare earth minerals aa secret weapon to counter the effects of tariffs from the United States",
              "United States Secretary Mike Pompeo said when he was asked about the threat of rare earth that the Americans have already \"lost and suffered for decades under the current rules\" and that the President's \"singular focus is to push back\" on China.\n\nHe also said that if it's the case that the Chinese Communist Party wanted to get information from technology that was in the possession of Huawei, it is almost certainly the case that Huawei would provide that to them"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "map": []
      },
      "sources": {
        "https://www.scmp.com/news/china/diplomacy/article/3012287/chinese-media-raise-rare-earths-threats-after-us-ups-trade-war": {
          "title": "China issues veiled rare earths threats after US ups trade war ...",
          "hostname": "www.scmp.com",
          "age": [
            "Thursday, May 30, 2019",
            "2019-05-30",
            "2632 days ago",
